 <h1 id="layers">Three Layers</h1>
 
-<p>The architecture keeps three things separate that most systems collapse into one. Collapsing them is what forces a new chain for every product.</p>
+<p>The architecture separates the claim, the admissible obligation transition, and the TemplatePack. Most systems combine them, which forces a new chain for every product.</p>
 
-<p>The <strong>conceptual</strong> primitive is the claim: a future cash flow with a probability distribution over its timing and amount, together with the terms that define the payoff, the authority that may issue or amend it, the evidence that resolves its state, and the rulebook that governs who may hold it. This is the layer a regulator, a trustee, or a counterparty reasons about.</p>
+<p>The <strong>conceptual</strong> primitive is the claim: a future cash flow with a probability distribution over its timing and amount. It includes the payoff terms, the issuing or amending authority, the evidence that resolves its state, and the rulebook governing eligible holders. Regulators, trustees, and counterparties reason about the claim in these terms.</p>
 
-<p>The <strong>protocol</strong> primitive is the admissible obligation transition: a state change in a claim&rsquo;s lifecycle that moves economic authority &mdash; issuance, attestation, transfer, margin update, netting, settlement, amendment, dispute, recovery, release, or revocation. A transition is admissible only when its authority, evidence, compliance attestation, reserved risk, execution certificate, settlement instruction, receipt, and reliance rulebook all validate together. The transition, not a prose description of the asset, is what consensus commits &mdash; so an invalid state cannot masquerade as a tradeable claim.</p>
+<p>The <strong>protocol</strong> primitive is the admissible obligation transition: a state change in a claim&rsquo;s lifecycle that moves economic authority. Transitions include issuance, attestation, transfer, margin update, netting, settlement, amendment, dispute, recovery, release, and revocation. Admission requires joint validation of authority, evidence, compliance attestation, reserved risk, execution certificate, settlement instruction, receipt, and reliance rulebook. Consensus commits the validated transition, so an invalid state cannot enter the record as a tradeable claim.</p>
 
-<p>The <strong>product</strong> primitive is the TemplatePack: the rulebook and machine-readable parameters for a claim family &mdash; authority, evidence, eligible holders, settlement asset, risk model, dispute path, recovery path, reporting, and reliance. A new product family adds a TemplatePack and a lowering into admissible transitions. It does not add a new root transaction type, a new settlement grammar, or a new compliance model.</p>
